Example 21 -- a GPU problem as one loadable module
===================================================

One runtime-loadable module, libGGPUParaboloid.so, carries both an optimization individual
(a paraboloid, GGPUParaboloid.hpp) and its GPU marshaller (GGPUParaboloidMarshaller.hpp). The
program GGPUParaboloidOptimizer has nothing problem-specific compiled in: courtier's launcher loads
the module, the individual claims the content-creator slot, the marshaller registers for the
device target "cuda", and --consumer gpu then evaluates on the device. The kernel
(kernels/paraboloid.cu) is compiled at run time with NVRTC. Requires a CUDA device for
--consumer gpu.

Files
-----
  GGPUParaboloidOptimizer.cpp   the generic program
  GGPUParaboloid.hpp            the individual
  GGPUParaboloidMarshaller.hpp  its marshaller for the device kernel
  GGPUParaboloidPlugin.cpp      the module's entry point: one manifest with two contributions
                                (the individual and the marshaller)
  kernels/paraboloid.cu         the device kernel, compiled at run time

Running
-------
  ./GGPUParaboloidOptimizer --module ./libGGPUParaboloid.so --consumer gpu   # on the device
  ./GGPUParaboloidOptimizer --module ./libGGPUParaboloid.so --consumer stc   # on the CPU

Example 14 has the same shape for a larger problem; example 20 does the same for an optimization
algorithm, example 18 for an individual without a GPU.
